 {:seon.task/name "a-set-holding-nil-called-as-a-function-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the Tests session on jolt/tests-items (2026-11-11). ``(#{nil :deferred} status)`` answers the element it finds, so for a nil status it answers nil, which reads as false: ```seon.agent/judge!``` and ```seon.agent/with-gate-verdicts``` took a test with no :seon.test/status for a judged one, so a process proof the gate never ran read as reused and green (seon.agent-test/a-process-test-gets-its-verdict-while-the-agent-can-act, red once its run stopped writing :deferred first). The two sites now say ``(contains? #{nil :deferred} status)``. What allowed it: nothing refuses a set literal holding nil or false in a call's head, though it can never answer true for that element. Correct: a derived class of ```seon.problems/health```, read from the compiler's facts as :unnamed-site is (docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon): each form calling a set literal (or a set def'd as a value) whose elements include nil or false is a problem naming its var, so the class cannot land again. The sweep on 2026-11-11 found these two sites only (src and test)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-set-holding-nil-called-as-a-function-is-a-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wiki-line-naming-what-no-longer-exists-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-11 (by Point): \"I want to move away from just putting all the information in one place and into distributing the updates to bring the system up to date before each session is concluded. Over time I want to stop drift.\" A session's sweep before it concludes is the rule; this is its script. What exists: a task's plan cites in triple backticks, and a dead cite is refused at its write (seon.task); a wiki section cites vars the same way (:seon.doc/names), and ```seon.problems/stale-sections``` makes a :stale-section row when a cited var's contract or docstring changed since the section was read. Nothing finds a wiki line naming a var that is gone, a config key no schema declares, a bin/check step ```seon.proofs/check-steps``` does not list, or a bin/ script or file that is not there: the sweep of 2026-11-11 found seon.prove's ns doc citing docs/proofs.md#the-test-service, a page that does not exist. Correct: a derived class of ```seon.problems/health``` beside :stale-section, read from the facts it already holds (the sections' names, the program's vars, the config schema's keys, check-steps, the tree's files at the checkout): each name in a section, a docstring or a schema :description that resolves to nothing is a problem row naming the line's address and the name, so its fix is the page edit. A docs/<page>.md#<anchor> address cited anywhere (src, test, the wiki, a task's plan) resolves to a heading of that page (seon.doc's anchors), not only to the page: Optimization's helper cited docs/merging.md#the-merge-gate, which laws and health passed and only a review caught (2026-11-11). A fenced form in a section whose head names a var is that var at an arity it has; evaluating a whole example form needs a world, so it is a test over the sections (seon.doc-test's), not health: the owner's round ruled every wiki example form evaluates (Point, 2026-11-11, Tests with Data model). Names in single backticks that are not a ns/var, :ns/key, bin/ path or docs/ address are words, never read. Cost: one pass over the sections' text and the descriptions at health (docs/health.md#cost). Proof: a section naming a removed var, an undeclared :seon.config key, an unlisted step and a missing docs/ page is four rows; the wiki at main is none, or each row is a fix landed with the task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-wiki-line-naming-what-no-longer-exists-is-a-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-entry-that-says-nothing-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a public function whose docstring cites no section is a problem of the branch that changed it (```seon.problems/uncited-vars```, docs/health.md#a-function-that-cites-no-section); a schema entry with no `:description` is nothing, and about 130 attributes of the entity schemas said what they are only in comments until branch jolt/descriptions. A count pinned in a test was tried and dropped: a second mechanism beside this one, with a number kept by hand. Plan: the same class for a schema entry, judged on what a branch changed: an entry of a schema file the branch added or changed that has no `:description` is a row, closed by writing one; added to the classes a branch is judged on. Cost: one seek a changed entry. Proof, red first: a branch that adds an attribute with no description has one row naming it; with a description, none; an entry the branch did not touch is not read."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-changed-schema-entry-with-no-description-is-a-row}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-costs-what-the-merge-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests. Found 2026-11-12 (tests, a whole prove after a merge of main): bin/check health ran STEP-OVERRAN at its 86.3 s bound (the step-end factor times its median, seon.proofs/step-bounds) at load 14; its sample shows the main thread working, not waiting. On jolt/tests its wall the same day ranged 21 s to 218 s: a run on a tree whose derived problems were made before is fast, the first run after a merge does the whole program's work again. So the bound is right and the cost is wrong (AGENTS.md section 3a.2, proportional to what changed): a merge of d changed forms must cost health O(d). Plan: measure first where the cold run's ticks go (the program db is already brought current by seon.source/refresh-tx from the nearest kept tree, test-runner/program-db, so look at what health derives over it: seon.problems' derived rows, the reach graph, the wiki's citations), estimate each part's lower bound from the forms the merge changed, then derive each from the previous tree's result plus the changed forms' facts, keyed by digest. Do not raise the bound. Proof, red first: health on a tree one changed form past a tree it already judged spends at most 10 times that form's own share of ticks, not the whole program's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/health-after-a-one-form-change-costs-that-form}}
 {:seon.task/name "problem-class-groups-are-schema-properties"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.problems' counted-classes and ended-classes (src/seon/problems.clj:37,39) list problem classes by hand, while caused-classes is already read from :seon.problems/class properties. Give each class its property in the :seon.problems/class enum (as :seon.problems/caused is) and read both groups from it; delete the two vectors. :task-conflict stays (fed by the one merge's conflicts on a task's attributes). Root approved, 2026-11-14 (data's hand-made-lists inventory)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-class-is-counted-and-ended-by-its-schema-property}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-forms-hand-made-lists-are-read-at-its-import"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by context building the hand-list rule (2026-11-14) [M]: seon.problems/hand-written-lists reads and walks every var's form (seon.source/read-forms, literal-lists) on each whole-program health read, ~4 contracted calls a var (seon.problems-test/health-reads-each-part-once), though the import reads each form once already. Done once, kept with its source (AGENTS.md section 3a.3): the import keeps a form's hand-made lists on the form (an attribute with its schema entry, data's), and the rule reads them by seek, a call a var at most. Proof: health-reads-each-part-once's :hand-written-list bound at (+ 1 n-vars) or under.\n\nMeasured by web, 2026-11-14 20:55-21:00 [M] (merged in from seon.problems/the-hand-written-list-class-reads-each-form-once, now cancelled):\n- On the standing root's main, seon.problems/health whole takes 80.4 s, and this class over every var is 77.7 s of it (n = 5,991 vars with a :var/form). The render proc read it whole inside its step and froze the standing root's pages.\n- Split on 300 vars: scan-form 3,222 ms and ~25 GB, literal-lists 8 ms. One 1,190-char form: scan-form 6.4 ms and 57 MB, all of it its :: regex (see Decided below), not the read.\nBuilt by web (root, 21:10); context-4 reviews the class, data-2 the import.\n- Plan: [:form/lists :lists] joins seon.program's form-sets, computed in the import's account from the form it already read (literal-lists moves into seon.program).\n- One fill of the existing forms at boot: each form's text read in its loaded namespace (read-string, ~0.03 ms), the reader's scan path only where its namespace isn't loaded; ~0.4 s once per store.\n- The class seeks :form/lists.\nEstimate: the class costs a seek of the :form/lists datoms (a few hundred, ms), and a whole health read goes from 80 s to ~3 s. Proofs: health-reads-each-part-once with :hand-written-list bounded by 1 + its rows, and seon.program-test/a-forms-hand-made-lists-are-recorded-at-import (red first).\n\nDecided 21:35 (web-2, root's question) [M on one 1,190-char form]: the cost was scan-form's regex rewrite (the lookbehind (?<![\\w:])::, 7.3 ms and 57 MB; irregex O(n²), fixed in deps' pin), not the read: seon.source/read-forms alone takes 0.14 ms and 35 KB. A whole hand-list scan without the regex is ~6,000 forms × ~0.17 ms ≈ 1 s, and as a health track it reruns only when a form moves. A stored :form/lists would save that ~1 s at the price of a schema attribute, an import walk and a boot fill, so it is DROPPED (90/10).\nThis task is done when context's Land A (scan-form reading with read-string) and deps' regex fix are on main and health-reads-each-part-once's :hand-written-list bound holds. Its proof seon.program-test/a-forms-hand-made-lists-are-recorded-at-import is withdrawn.\n\nAfter context's Land A (4c386d46d3: the regex is seon.problems/unaliased, one pass, ~5 ticks a char; a form's read ~25 ticks a char) [M, live main, 21:45]: hand-written-lists over every var takes 1,368 ms and 929 MB for 544 rows, from 77.7 s. This task's need is met: no stored :form/lists. It is done when health-reads-each-part-once's :hand-written-list bound holds on main; as a health track it reruns only on moved forms (seon.render.proc/a-whole-health-read-never-holds-the-render-proc)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/health-reads-each-part-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-problem-class-files-its-rows-through-its-template"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by tests' dead-code audit (2026-11-14, root's ruling: owner-ruled, \"fixes filed as easy agent tasks\") [M, grep]: seon.template/focus-docstring builds the task asking an agent to focus one var's docstring, and docs/health.md:79 says unfocused docstrings close through it, one task a var; nothing calls it, and seon.problems/unfocused-docstrings raises the row without filing it. Two other classes file their rows as tasks, each wired its own way: slow tests (my.root/slow-tests-tx, seon.template/slow-test) and stalls (seon.system's stall task, seon.template/stall). One mechanism for all: a class's entry in :seon.problems/class names its template (a property, :seon.problems/template, with its schema entry), and one filer files a task per open row the class answers, skipping a row whose task the db holds; the slow-test and stall wirings move onto it (their behaviour unchanged) (tests agreed 2026-11-14 for my.root/slow-tests-tx: its own filing path deleted in the same landing, its rows and task text unchanged, its existing test green as the proof; tests reviews that diff) and :unfocused-docstring gains focus-docstring's task. No functionality removed. Proof: an open :unfocused-docstring row files exactly one focus-docstring task, a second filing files none, and the slow-test and stall rows file what they filed before."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-problem-class-files-its-rows-through-its-template}}
 {:seon.task/name "laws-and-health-are-one-set-of-live-classes"
  :seon.task/plan "Web's design task (root approved the shape, 2026-11-15; data reviews; runtime for the owner step). Owner, verbatim via root: \"schedule laws and health checks to be rewritten (reimagined first with seon first designs that leverage the system NOT ports). Convert any bins to thin wrappers and maybe even rename and combine the tools if they are duplciating work. Disable blocking tools that aren't adding value and just build the right solution inside seon instead.\"\n\nWhat exists [M]:\n- seon.problems/laws is data. catch and printer are already rows of it, read into the :unnamed-site class over the compiler's facts.\n- bin/laws text-scans eight marks: catch, printer, clocks, measures, resolution, transactions, dates, defs. Two of them duplicate the class.\n- health (the full path) and health-moved are two implementations of one value. caused-split gives a branch's own problems.\n- The render proc keeps health live and answers {:op :health}.\n- The T2 trial: a class as a reaction took 247 ms first and 0 ms untouched.\n- seon.db/reads-add is an in-process read index with no production caller.\n\nMeasured [M]:\n- bin/laws 9.4 s; bin/check health 10.2 s cold.\n- In root: full health 168M ticks / 462 MB per main move, against health-moved's 8.2M.\n- In a land under load: laws 31 s, health 19 s, of ~975 s of check steps. The minutes are the e2e steps.\n\nDisabling today would lose the defs and dates gating, and each unsanctioned clocks, measures, resolution or transactions site. So no scan goes until its law is a class.\n\nDesign:\n- S1: refs record a host static as Class/member, seon.program/a-form-records-the-host-members-and-vars-it-calls (runtime; data reviews). No new attribute (data-2's review: one form fact per kind of reference).\n- S2: every law a row of seon.problems/laws (clocks and measures by the host statics in :form/refs; resolution by :form/refs less :form/quoted; transactions by :form/refs; dates by the moved form's text, :form/code, so a date in a code string is found as bin/laws finds it; defs by two live forms whose :form/defines name one var), each deleting its bin/laws scan in the same landing.\n- S3: every class a reaction over the program facts it reads, through the one read index (seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved). health, health-moved and render.proc's full fallback collapse into it. A branch's problems are the classes its moved datoms reach (caused-split, read off the same reactions).\n- S4: one op, {:op :health} and {:op :health :branch b}. bin/check health becomes a bin/mcp call; bin/laws is deleted; bin/land's two steps become that one call.\n\nEstimates: a class reads the forms carrying its fact (aevt), O(moved forms) a move; the first read is the whole classes, then O(classes reached).\n\nProofs to write, each red first:\n- a main move touching no class's keys reruns none;\n- a commit adding an unsanctioned catch reruns :unnamed-site alone;\n- each moved law finds the site its scan found.\n\nFull plan: the session notes, web-task-plan-laws-and-health-live-2026-11-15.md.\n\nS2 row added (root's ruling, 2026-11-15 08:20; flow defines the rule and reviews the first sweep's declarations): :unended-wait. Each wait site in src (a deref of a promise, <!!, >!!, alts!!, await-reply, ask-edge, await-db; the wait vars a spec'd dial) is in a var declaring {:seon.flow/ends #{...}} from one enum (:answer :typed-error :eof :stop :interrupt :bound :ticks :deadlock). A site with none declared, or with :answer alone, is a row. It reuses :stateful-outside-flow's ref pass over :form/refs, with no new fact. Red first: a deref with nothing declared is a row; #{:answer :stop} is none. Definition: the session notes, flow-law-every-wait-names-its-ends-2026-11-15.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/a-form-records-the-host-members-and-vars-it-calls" "seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved"}}
]
